WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.095
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.395 --> 00:00:08.572
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.872 --> 00:00:16.679
Unit thirteen: Economics of the Future. In this unit, you will debate alternative visions of economic progress.

4
00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:18.812
post-growth.

5
00:00:19.512 --> 00:00:26.318
post-growth means describing an economy focused on wellbeing rather than constant expansion.

6
00:00:26.618 --> 00:00:30.951
Post-growth thinkers question GDP as the measure of success.

7
00:00:31.651 --> 00:00:34.687
Your turn. Say: post-growth.

8
00:00:37.387 --> 00:00:38.527
post-growth.

9
00:00:39.517 --> 00:00:40.332
degrowth.

10
00:00:41.032 --> 00:00:45.980
degrowth means a planned reduction of production and consumption in rich countries.

11
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.360
Degrowth remains controversial among economists.

12
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:53.652
Your turn. Say: degrowth.

13
00:00:56.352 --> 00:00:57.214
degrowth.

14
00:00:58.217 --> 00:01:00.007
circular economy model.

15
00:01:00.707 --> 00:01:06.666
circular economy model means a system that reuses and recycles materials instead of wasting them.

16
00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:10.277
The circular economy model designs out waste.

17
00:01:10.977 --> 00:01:14.338
Your turn. Say: circular economy model.

18
00:01:17.038 --> 00:01:18.840
circular economy model.

19
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.047
universal basic services.

20
00:01:22.747 --> 00:01:29.425
universal basic services means free public provision of essentials such as health, education and transport.

21
00:01:29.725 --> 00:01:32.874
Universal basic services could reduce inequality.

22
00:01:33.574 --> 00:01:37.724
Your turn. Say: universal basic services.

23
00:01:40.424 --> 00:01:42.493
universal basic services.

24
00:01:43.483 --> 00:01:45.088
automation dividend.

25
00:01:45.788 --> 00:01:52.014
automation dividend means the extra wealth produced by automation, potentially shared with society.

26
00:01:52.314 --> 00:01:54.743
Who should receive the automation dividend?

27
00:01:55.443 --> 00:01:58.652
Your turn. Say: automation dividend.

28
00:02:01.352 --> 00:02:03.027
automation dividend.

29
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:05.424
digital currency.

30
00:02:06.124 --> 00:02:10.132
digital currency means money that exists only in electronic form.

31
00:02:10.432 --> 00:02:13.639
Several central banks are testing a digital currency.

32
00:02:14.339 --> 00:02:17.456
Your turn. Say: digital currency.

33
00:02:20.156 --> 00:02:21.424
digital currency.

34
00:02:22.417 --> 00:02:23.511
central bank.

35
00:02:24.211 --> 00:02:30.355
central bank means the national institution that controls money supply and interest rates.

36
00:02:30.655 --> 00:02:33.107
The central bank raised interest rates.

37
00:02:33.807 --> 00:02:36.866
Your turn. Say: central bank.

38
00:02:39.566 --> 00:02:40.776
central bank.

39
00:02:41.783 --> 00:02:43.411
quantitative easing.

40
00:02:44.111 --> 00:02:49.861
quantitative easing means a central bank buying assets to inject money into the economy.

41
00:02:50.161 --> 00:02:54.784
Quantitative easing was used after the 2008 crisis.

42
00:02:55.484 --> 00:02:59.147
Your turn. Say: quantitative easing.

43
00:03:01.847 --> 00:03:03.428
quantitative easing.

44
00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:06.602
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:06.902 --> 00:03:09.180
Rithy, must economies always grow?

46
00:03:10.483 --> 00:03:14.146
Most economists would say so. Degrowth advocates wouldn't.

47
00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:16.428
Why not?

48
00:03:17.717 --> 00:03:25.025
Because, they argue, endless growth on a finite planet is impossible. Wellbeing, not output, should be the goal.

49
00:03:26.317 --> 00:03:28.281
And the circular economy model?

50
00:03:29.583 --> 00:03:34.651
More widely accepted. Waste it designs out; value it keeps in use.

51
00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:40.077
What about money itself? Central banks are testing digital currencies.

52
00:03:41.383 --> 00:03:45.847
Some already have. Others will, I suspect, sooner rather than later.

53
00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:51.286
Grammar focus: Ellipsis, substitution and fronting.

54
00:03:51.586 --> 00:03:56.337
Combine ellipsis, substitution and fronting for elegant contrast.

55
00:03:56.637 --> 00:04:00.381
Most economists would say so. Degrowth advocates wouldn't.

56
00:04:00.681 --> 00:04:04.352
Waste it designs out; value it keeps in use.

57
00:04:04.652 --> 00:04:10.657
Combining fronting with ellipsis creates elegant, balanced contrasts typical of skilled writers.

58
00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:14.430
Now read and listen. Beyond Growth?.

59
00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:20.015
For decades, economic success has been measured mainly by growth in GDP.

60
00:04:21.317 --> 00:04:27.368
Growth has lifted billions out of poverty; whether it can continue indefinitely, many now doubt.

61
00:04:28.683 --> 00:04:33.794
Post-growth economists argue that rich countries should prioritise wellbeing over output.

62
00:04:35.083 --> 00:04:39.660
Degrowth advocates go further, calling for planned reductions in consumption.

63
00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:47.535
Critics reply that poorer countries need growth, and that technology can decouple it from emissions.

64
00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:55.540
The circular economy model offers a middle path, keeping materials in use rather than discarding them.

65
00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:05.514
Meanwhile, central banks, having used quantitative easing after 2008, are now experimenting with digital currencies.

66
00:05:06.817 --> 00:05:12.311
The economy of the future, whatever its shape, will need to serve both people and planet.

67
00:05:13.617 --> 00:05:16.513
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:16.813 --> 00:05:20.124
A central bank buying assets to inject money is blank.

69
00:05:20.424 --> 00:05:25.889
A, quantitative easing. B, degrowth. C, digital currency.

70
00:05:29.883 --> 00:05:32.870
The answer is A: quantitative easing.

71
00:05:34.383 --> 00:05:37.514
'Will prices rise?' 'I expect blank.'

72
00:05:37.814 --> 00:05:40.678
A, it. B, so. C, that.

73
00:05:44.683 --> 00:05:46.509
The answer is B: so.

74
00:05:48.017 --> 00:05:51.398
Some countries have adopted it; others haven't blank.

75
00:05:51.698 --> 00:05:54.376
A, yet. B, it. C, done.

76
00:05:58.383 --> 00:06:00.476
The answer is A: yet.

77
00:06:01.983 --> 00:06:06.572
A system that reuses materials instead of wasting them is the blank.

78
00:06:06.872 --> 00:06:11.965
A, circular economy model. B, post-growth. C, central bank.

79
00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:19.064
The answer is A: circular economy model.

80
00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:24.326
According to the text, what do critics say poorer countries need?

81
00:06:24.626 --> 00:06:30.149
A, degrowth. B, growth. C, less technology.

82
00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:36.370
The answer is B: growth.

83
00:06:37.883 --> 00:06:43.450
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should rich countries stop pursuing economic growth?

84
00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:54.325
Part two: Use ellipsis or fronting to contrast two views, then conclude.

85
00:07:00.625 --> 00:07:12.905
Degrowth advocates say they should; most economists say they shouldn't. Growth poor countries need; wealthy ones may not, at least not in its current form. I'd favour greener growth over no growth at all.

86
00:07:13.205 --> 00:07:15.657
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:22.147
Great work today! You learned eight new words and ellipsis, substitution and fronting.

88
00:07:22.447 --> 00:07:27.804
Today's value is responsibility. Help at home and do your jobs.

89
00:07:28.104 --> 00:07:30.838
See you in Unit fourteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:31.138 --> 00:07:33.895
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

